EXTRA = [
    {"after": "teamwork", "id": "professionalism", "nav": "Professionalism", "tag": "Practice framework",
     "h2": "What professionalism means for a dialysis nurse",
     "html": """
<p>Professionalism is often described in terms of appearance: a clean uniform, a calm voice, a neat chart. Those matter, but they are the surface. Underneath, professionalism is the set of habits that lets patients and colleagues trust a nurse without checking: that the nurse will do what was promised, do it competently, admit what went wrong, and treat every person with the same respect. In a dialysis unit that trust is tested several times a week, with the same patients, for years.</p>
<p><strong>The legal and ethical frame.</strong> Republic Act No. 9173, the Philippine Nursing Act of 2002, defines the scope of nursing practice, places the profession under the Board of Nursing and the PRC, and allows the Board to discipline a registered nurse for unprofessional or unethical conduct.[[ra9173]] The Board of Nursing&rsquo;s Code of Ethics for Registered Nurses, promulgated by Board Resolution No. 220, Series of 2004, sets out the nurse&rsquo;s responsibilities to people, to practice, to coworkers, to society, and to the profession. Among other things it asks nurses to develop the competence their work requires, to keep working relationships with colleagues collegial and collaborative, and to decline gifts or favors that could be read as taking advantage of patients. A nurse does not need to memorize the code, but should have read it once and know where to find it.</p>
<p><strong>Four parts of professionalism.</strong></p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What it looks like on a dialysis shift</th><th>What erodes it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Accountability</td><td>Owning one&rsquo;s actions and their results: reporting a wrong dialyzer before the head nurse finds it, filing the incident report for one&rsquo;s own near miss, finishing the documentation one started</td><td>Hiding errors, blaming the machine or the previous shift, signing for work one did not see</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Arriving on time, prepared; doing the checks every time, not only when observed; following through on what was promised to a patient or colleague</td><td>Late arrival that delays the first shift, skipped checks on busy days, promises to patients that are forgotten</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competence</td><td>Working within one&rsquo;s training and sign-offs; asking for help with a skill not yet signed off; keeping knowledge current</td><td>Doing an unfamiliar procedure to avoid admitting inexperience; relying on what was taught years ago</td></tr>
<tr><td>Respect</td><td>The same courtesy to a patient who pays privately and one on charity; using the patient&rsquo;s preferred name; listening before explaining; courtesy to technicians, assistants, and cleaners</td><td>Talking about patients in front of others, scolding patients for weight gains, sarcasm toward junior staff</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Why dialysis tests professionalism.</strong> The work is repetitive, and repetition invites shortcuts: the hundredth cannulation feels routine, and the step that has never yet mattered is the one that gets skipped. Patients become familiar, and familiarity can slide into casual talk, favors, or impatience. The unit is small, so conflicts between staff are felt by everyone, including patients. A professional nurse notices these pressures and deliberately keeps standards the same on the fiftieth day as on the first.</p>
<p><strong>Accountability is not blame.</strong> A unit that punishes every reported error will stop receiving reports. The just-culture approach described later in this module distinguishes between honest mistakes, which are reported and learned from; at-risk behavior, such as a shortcut that has become normal, which is coached; and reckless disregard of a known risk, which is a disciplinary matter. Owning one&rsquo;s actions is easier when the unit treats it in this way, and each nurse helps to build that culture by reporting their own near misses first.</p>
<p><strong>Continuing competence.</strong> A license shows that a nurse was competent on the day of the examination. Staying competent is a continuing obligation. The PRC sets CPD requirements for license renewal, as described earlier in this module, and the unit sets its own competency checks for dialysis skills. Beyond these minimums, a professional nurse reads the unit&rsquo;s audit results, learns from incident reviews, keeps current with changes in practice such as new vaccines or new machines, and is willing to teach. When guidance changes, the professional response is to update practice, not to defend the old habit.</p>
<p><strong>Personal health and fitness for duty.</strong> A nurse who is ill, exhausted after a double shift, or impaired by alcohol or medicines is a risk to patients, however skilled. Reporting unfitness for duty and asking for relief is a professional act, not a weakness, and the unit&rsquo;s staffing plan should make it possible.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "professionalism", "id": "conduct", "nav": "Professional conduct", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "Professional conduct: boundaries, privacy, handoff, and speaking up",
     "html": """
<p>The ethics section later in this module covers gifts, borrowing, and private arrangements with patients. The points below extend those boundaries to other situations that arise in long-term dialysis care.</p>
<p><strong>Personal relationships.</strong> Warmth toward patients is part of good care; a personal relationship is not. Romantic or sexual relationships with a current patient are never acceptable. Friendships outside the unit, business dealings, and acting as a patient&rsquo;s private caregiver for pay all blur the line between nurse and friend, and make it harder to give that patient the same care and the same honest information as everyone else. If a nurse already knows a patient personally, for example a relative or a neighbor, the head nurse is told, so that assignments can be arranged where necessary.</p>
<p><strong>Money.</strong> Do not lend money to or borrow from patients or their families, sell them products, collect contributions, or accept payment for a preferred schedule, station, or machine. A patient who asks for help with costs is referred to the social worker and the facility&rsquo;s assistance processes.</p>
<p><strong>Social media and patient privacy.</strong> The Data Privacy Act treats health information as sensitive personal information.[[ra10173]] On social media, a post can identify a patient without naming them: a photograph showing a corner of the unit, a description of &ldquo;my patient with the big fistula who always arrives late&rdquo;, or a birthday greeting tagged from the unit&rsquo;s account. Do not post about patients, even without names or faces, and do not discuss workplace incidents online. Friend requests from patients are declined politely, under the facility&rsquo;s policy, and patient questions sent by private message are redirected to the unit&rsquo;s official channels. Remember that a nurse&rsquo;s personal posts about colleagues or the facility can also reflect on the profession.</p>
<p><strong>Punctuality and handoff.</strong> In a unit where the first shift begins early, a late nurse delays every patient on the shift, and each lost minute is a minute of treatment some patient will not receive or a delay to the next shift. Handoff at shift change is a high-risk moment. A professional handoff is given at the station or with the record in hand, covers each patient&rsquo;s current status, pending results, and concerns, and is not rushed because the outgoing nurse wants to leave. The receiving nurse asks questions until the picture is clear; responsibility passes only when the handoff is complete.</p>
<p><strong>Conflicts of interest.</strong> A conflict of interest exists when something the nurse gains could influence, or appear to influence, care. Examples include accepting meals or gifts from a company that supplies the unit, recommending a particular pharmacy, laboratory, or supplement in which the nurse has an interest, or receiving a fee for referring patients to another facility. The rule is to declare the interest to the head nurse and follow facility policy, and to let the patient&rsquo;s needs alone guide any recommendation.</p>
<p><strong>Peer accountability.</strong> Speaking up to a physician was covered earlier in this module. Speaking to a peer can be harder. A colleague who repeatedly skips hand hygiene between stations, documents checks not done, or appears impaired at work puts patients at risk. Start privately and directly with the fact and the concern, if it is safe to do so; if the behavior continues, or if a patient is in immediate danger, go to the head nurse. Covering for a colleague is not loyalty; it leaves the colleague without help and the patients without protection.</p>
<p><strong>Incivility and bullying.</strong> Eye-rolling, public criticism, sarcasm, exclusion, unfair assignments, and shouting are common in high-pressure units and are often directed at new nurses. They cause stress and departures, and they make staff less willing to ask questions or report errors, which harms patients. A professional nurse does not take part in them, does not stay silent when a colleague is targeted, and reports persistent bullying through the facility&rsquo;s process. Senior nurses set the tone: the way they answer a junior&rsquo;s question shows everyone whether questions are welcome.</p>
<p><strong>Cultural humility.</strong> Patients in a Filipino unit differ in language, region, religion, income, and beliefs about illness. Many use herbal remedies, consult a <em>manghihilot</em> or faith healer, or turn to prayer alongside dialysis. Cultural humility means asking rather than assuming, listening without ridicule, and recognizing one&rsquo;s own assumptions. Ask what the patient is taking or doing, explain any real safety concern, such as a herbal product that may raise potassium, and record and report it; do not mock the belief. Use the language the patient understands best, and arrange interpretation for important conversations when needed.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Professional response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A grateful patient offers a nurse an envelope with cash at Christmas</td><td>Decline warmly, thank the patient, and explain that staff cannot accept money; follow the facility policy on shared gifts for the team</td></tr>
<tr><td>A colleague posts a photograph from the unit on social media; a patient&rsquo;s arm and machine screen are visible</td><td>Ask the colleague to remove it at once, and inform the head nurse so that the privacy process can be followed</td></tr>
<tr><td>The nurse finishing the shift wants to hand off quickly in the corridor</td><td>Ask to do the handoff at the stations with the records, covering each patient&rsquo;s status, pending results, and concerns</td></tr>
<tr><td>A supplier representative offers a nurse a free phone for recommending its product</td><td>Decline, and report the offer to the head nurse under the conflict-of-interest policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>A colleague repeatedly records hand hygiene audits that did not take place</td><td>Raise it privately first if safe, then report to the head nurse; a false record is never covered</td></tr>
<tr><td>A senior nurse ridicules a new nurse in front of patients</td><td>Support the new nurse in the moment, speak to the senior nurse privately or report it, and use the facility&rsquo;s process if it continues</td></tr>
<tr><td>A patient says she drinks a herbal tea from her province to &ldquo;clean the kidneys&rdquo;</td><td>Ask what it is and how much, avoid ridicule, explain the concern about unknown herbal products in kidney failure, and report it to the physician</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
"""},
]
